Cell["7.  Loss curve and final result", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"46a42a3a-584c-492a-8296-d803dfa2d517"],

Cell["Import[\"figures/charlevel_loss_curve.png\"]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"1f576738-702f-488c-a3f3-4faeabd63ec0"],

Cell["\<\
The curve has the same shape Karpathy's char-rnn produced in 2015 at \
comparable scale: a fast initial drop in the first round (the model learns \
the character-frequency distribution), a slower elbow as it discovers word \
boundaries, and a long shallow tail where it refines local syntax.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"7a38806e-ae3d-45d9-8aa2-12c61b3e7480"],

Cell["\<\
We trained for 5.5 hours, completed 23 rounds, hit a final round loss of \
~0.74 (train perplexity ~2.1).  Held-out eval loss came out to 2.59 (eval \
perplexity 13.4), giving a train/eval gap of 3.49 \[LongDash] a clear overfit \
signature.  At this scale, with 1.8M parameters memorising a 1.08M-character \
corpus, the asymptote of the train loss is well below the asymptote of the \
eval loss; further training would push train down without making the model \
actually generalise better.  The checkpoint we ship is the one at the end of \
this run.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"d5e0df07-6353-4574-ba8d-8c1d6d7322fa"],

Cell["\<\
Import[\"data/charlevel_eval_summary.json\"][[
    {\"trainLoss\", \"trainPerplexity\", \"evalLoss\", \"evalPerplexity\",
     \"generalisationGap\", \"verdict\"}]]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"39a3f006-a560-4dbf-9496-ebf989b926e5"],

Cell["8.  Summary", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"037c0f76-8c47-44c7-8021-09199c1ad49a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\[FilledSquare] ",
 StyleBox["A trained char-level transformer",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " on Tiny Shakespeare (1.8M parameters), saved to ",
 StyleBox["data/charlevel_shakespeare_trained.wlnet",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 ".  Reusable in Parts 5\[Dash]8.\n",
 "\[FilledSquare] ",
 StyleBox["A complete training loop",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " (",
 StyleBox["trainGptModel",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 ") that auto-wraps the model in a softmax for proper cross-entropy ",
 "computation, ships a cosine + linear-warmup schedule, and saves a ",
 "checkpoint every five minutes.\n",
 "\[FilledSquare] ",
 StyleBox["A bug postmortem",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " on the silent softmax-omission failure: when your cross-entropy ",
 "loss goes negative, look at the model output.\n",
 "\[FilledSquare] ",
 StyleBox["A training-in-progress gallery",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " of generations evolving across rounds (Section 6).\n",
 "\[FilledSquare] ",
 StyleBox["A cloud-submission path",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " (",
 StyleBox["submitRemotePretraining",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 ") for scaling to the 25M or 124M tiers on Wolfram Cloud."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"2baf2bd0-2466-4d35-a1b2-937173d5dc23"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In ",
 StyleBox["Part 5",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " we look at the inference side of this model: greedy decoding, ",
 "temperature sampling, top-k, top-p, beam search, and speculative ",
 "decoding."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"644682c7-01dc-4880-a19c-7a616dc94f91"],

Cell["References", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"a4de62ca-8a76-4da5-8292-9f8429c8d3cb"],

Cell["\<\
Karpathy, A. (2015). The Unreasonable Effectiveness of Recurrent Neural \
Networks.
Hoffmann, J. et al. (2022). Training Compute-Optimal Large Language Models. \
(Chinchilla.)
Karpathy, A. (2025). nanochat.
Raschka, S. (2024). Build a Large Language Model from Scratch. Manning.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"3ca05ca2-55c0-48f6-80ac-925f4bde62dc"]
},
